{"t":1792890556,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"0ee1abc1-add7-47e4-8830-fff251826e48","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-24T23:30:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 242330\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n730 PM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nFrost Advisories remain in effect for portions of northern Virginia \nand central/northern Maryland early Sunday morning. Outside of these \nadvisories, the current forecast package remains on track. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Another chilly night ahead with Frost Advisories in effect for \nportions of northern Virginia and central/northern Maryland. \n\n- 2) Seasonable temperatures and dry conditions continue through \nmuch of next week. \n\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another chilly night ahead with Frost \nAdvisories in effect for portions of northern Virginia and \ncentral/northern Maryland. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to meander over the area this evening \nbefore drifting southwest Sunday into Monday. As a result, expect a \ncontinuation of frost and freeze conditions during the overnight \nperiods with seasonable temperatures during the daylight hours. \n\nTonight appears to be the coldest night of the next few given high \npressure directly overhead. Hi-res CAM guidance continues to show \npersistence in regards to widespread frost and freeze conditions \ntonight, especially in locations west of I-95. Temperatures look to \nbe fairly similar to what we saw this morning in the 31 -36 degree \nrange with slightly warmer temperatures over the metros and colder \ntemperatures (upper 20s) over the mountains. With that said, Frost \nAdvisories have been issued for portions of northern VA and \ncentral/northern MD from 1 to 9am Sunday. Frost will be scattered in \nnature given the decision of an advisory vs. warning across these \nlocations. \n\nThese locations are also still in the growing season for 2026 while \nlocations west of the Blue Ridge are no longer active. Locations \neast of the advisory (along and east of I-95) will also have a \nchance at some frost in the colder pockets, but should remain \nslightly warmer given the Chesapeake Bay influence. Confidence \nremains lower for an advisory expansion into central \nVirginia/southern MD due to higher dew points and potential cloud \ncover, as a remnant stratocumulus field continues to drift westward \nand possibly banking along the Blue Ridge overnight into early \nSunday morning.\n\nHigh pressure will gradually drift southwest toward the central \nAppalachians Sunday night into Monday morning. Low level flow will \nturn westerly allowing for less of a marine influence Sunday into \nSunday night. This should set us up for one more good radiational \ncooling night (Sunday night) before temperatures warm under return \nflow early in the workweek. Sunday night will be more of a ridge-\nvalley split type of night with cooler conditions in the \nvalleys/cold pockets/locations along and east of I-95 compared to \nthe ridge tops given the sharp surface inversion/decoupling. \n\nOnly minor changes have been made to the previous min temperature \nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10th percentile and \nCanadian Regional).\n\nExpect temperatures to rise above normal (upper 60s to lower 70s) by \nMonday afternoon as the warming airmass mixes to the surface. \nMinimal cloud cover and light winds will remain persistent.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures and dry conditions\ncontinue through much of next week. \n\nDry weather continues into the midweek period as surface high \npressure lingers to the south Tuesday and Wednesday. A backdoor dry \nfrontal boundary associated with a low over the Northern Plains \nlikely traverses through the region during this time, but no \nprecipitation is anticipated from this as moisture/PWATs remain low. \nAt most, cloud cover begins to increase during this period, with \ndaytime temperatures approaching the mid-70s as high pressure \npersists.  \n\nLooking ahead, a weak disturbance over the Ohio River Valley \npotentially moves through the Mid-Atlantic late in the week. Impacts \nfrom this look minimal at this time, as PWATs continue to range on \nthe dry side while winds remain light. Overall, no precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated for the entire 7-day forecast period at this \ntime. Dry conditions continue to prevail with seasonable \ntemperatures ranging in the 60s-70s during the day, with overnight \nlows mostly in the 40s throughout the week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nPrevailing VFR conditions continue through early next week with \nbroad high pressure nearby. A slight uptick in low level moisture \nwill lead to an uptick in stratocumulus this evening east of the \nBlue Ridge along with the potential for some patchy river valley fog \nearly Sunday/Monday mornings. Confidence overall is low in the \ncoverage and timing of fog with a focus on Monday morning compared \nto Sunday morning given the lastest hi-res CAM guidance. \n\nWinds will remain light and variable tonight before switching \nsoutheast to northeast SUnday as high pressure drifts overhead. \nWinds will change back to the northeast Monday with speeds both days \nless than 10kts. \n\nVFR conditions are expected to continue through Thursday next week. \nWinds shift north to northwesterly Tuesday in the wake of a dry cold \nfront, then shift east to southeasterly between Wednesday and \nThursday. Cloud cover could begin to increase somewhat between \nWednesday/Thursday as a frontal boundary approaches, but no \nprecipitation is anticipated at this time.\n\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNo marine hazards are expected through Monday with \nhigh pressure overhead. Winds will vary a bit from northeast to \nsoutheast Sunday before becoming westerly to northwesterly \nMonday. Speeds will remain less than 10 kts. \n\nA dry backdoor cold front passing through could bring wind gusts \nnear SCA criteria on Tuesday before diminishing again overnight. \nNortherly winds shift easterly Wednesday in the wake of the front.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nGenerally light but steady east to southeast winds are expected to \nkeep water levels elevated through Sunday. When paired with high \nastronomical tides due to the upcoming full moon on October 26, \nadditional rounds of minor tidal flooding are possible. Annapolis \nhas a chance of reaching minor flood stage Sunday afternoon. \nGuidance does show a more notable decline in water levels by Monday \nwhen flow finally turns westerly.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Sunday for MDZ004>006-008-\n     503-505-507-508.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 8 PM EDT this evening for MDZ014.\nVA...Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Sunday for VAZ040-051-501-\n     502-505-506-526-527.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Sunday for ANZ530.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/DHOF/EST/SRT\nAVIATION...EST/SRT\nMARINE...EST/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792890527,"days":{"2026-10-24":{"hi":null,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Clear"},"2026-10-25":{"hi":67,"lo":43,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-26":{"hi":71,"lo":50,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-27":{"hi":69,"lo":48,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":67,"lo":49,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":72,"lo":50,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":75,"lo":52,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":73,"lo":null,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1792890000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,nu